  "question": "(a) $\\int_{-6}^{5} f(x)\\,dx = \\int_{-6}^{-2} f(x)\\,dx + \\int_{-2}^{5} f(x)\\,dx$\n\n(b) $\\int_{3}^{5} (2f'(x) + 4)\\,dx$\n\n(c) Let $g(x) = \\int_{-6}^{x} f(t)\\,dt$. Find the absolute maximum value of $g$ on the interval $[-2, 5]$.\n\n(d) $\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{10^{x} - 3f'(x)}{f(x) - \\arctan x}$",
  "solution": "(a) $\\int_{-6}^{5} f(x)\\,dx = \\int_{-6}^{-2} f(x)\\,dx + \\int_{-2}^{5} f(x)\\,dx$\n\n$\\Rightarrow 7 = \\int_{-6}^{-2} f(x)\\,dx + 2 + \\left(9 - \\frac{9\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n\n$\\Rightarrow \\int_{-6}^{-2} f(x)\\,dx = 7 - \\left(11 - \\frac{9\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{9\\pi}{4} - 4$\n\n(b) $\\int_{3}^{5} (2f'(x) + 4)\\,dx = 2\\int_{3}^{5} f'(x)\\,dx + \\int_{3}^{4} 4\\,dx$\n\n$= 2(f(5) - f(3)) + 4(5 - 3)$\n\n$= 2(0 - (3 - \\sqrt{5})) + 8$\n\n$= 2(-3 + \\sqrt{5}) + 8 = 2 + 2\\sqrt{5}$\n\n$-\\text{OR}-$\n\n$\\int_{3}^{5} (2f'(x) + 4)\\,dx = [2f(x) + 4x]_{x=3}^{x=5}$\n\n$= (2f(5) + 20) - (2f(3) + 12)$\n\n$= (2 \\cdot 0 + 20) - (2(3 - \\sqrt{5}) + 12)$\n\n$= 2 + 2\\sqrt{5}$\n\n(c) $g'(x) = f(x) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = -1, x = \\frac{1}{2}, x = 5$\n\nTable of critical points and endpoints:\n| $x$ | $g(x)$ |\n|-----|--------|\n| $-2$ | $0$ |\n| $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $5$ | $11 - \\frac{9\\pi}{4}$ |\n\nOn the interval $-2 \\le x \\le 5$, the absolute maximum value of $g$ is $g(5) = 11 - \\frac{9\\pi}{4}$.\n\n(d) $\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{10^{x} - 3f'(x)}{f(x) - \\arctan x} = \\frac{10^{1} - 3f'(1)}{f(1) - \\arctan 1}$\n\n$= \\frac{10 - 3 \\cdot 2}{1 - \\arctan 1}$\n\n$= \\frac{4}{1 - \\arctan 1} = \\frac{4}{1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}}$",
  "grading_scheme": "Part (a): 3 points\n  1 point: $\\int_{-6}^{5} f(x)\\,dx = \\int_{-6}^{-2} f(x)\\,dx + \\int_{-2}^{5} f(x)\\,dx$\n  1 point: $\\int_{-2}^{5} f(x)\\,dx$\n  1 point: answer\n\nPart (b): 2 points\n  1 point: Fundamental Theorem of Calculus\n  1 point: answer\n\nPart (c): 3 points\n  1 point: $g'(x) = f(x)$\n  1 point: identifies $x = -1$ as a candidate\n  1 point: answer with justification\n\nPart (d): 1 point\n  1 point: answer",
